Yeah—trope is the broad word, but there are a few neighboring terms:

A trope is a recognizable storytelling pattern. A plot device is a mechanism that moves the story. An archetype is a recurring type of character, role, or situation. A motif is a repeated image, phrase, object, or idea. A cliché is usually a trope that’s been used so predictably it feels stale.

Some of the most common ones:

* MacGuffin — the thing everyone is chasing; its real purpose is to make the plot move.
* Chosen One — an apparently ordinary person is destined for an extraordinary role. Harry Potter, Neo, Luke Skywalker.
* Hero’s Journey — departure → trials → transformation → return. Probably the giant granddaddy pattern behind adventure stories.
* Mentor — the older/experienced figure who equips the hero, then often disappears or dies. Obi-Wan, Gandalf, Morpheus.
* Threshold Guardian — someone or something that tests whether the protagonist is ready to enter the next world/stage.
* Call to Adventure / Refusal of the Call — opportunity arrives; hero initially says no. Very common because reluctance makes commitment meaningful.
* The Quest — “go there, get/do the thing.” The external journey usually disguises an internal one.
* Treasure Was the Friends We Made Along the Way — characters pursue an object or goal and discover the real gain was relationship/transformation. Exactly adjacent to what we were just discussing.
* Chekhov’s Gun — if a conspicuous thing is introduced, audiences expect it to matter later. Narrative conservation of detail.
* Red Herring — something deliberately made to look important so attention goes the wrong way.
* Foreshadowing — an early detail whose significance only becomes clear later.
* Callback — something from earlier returns later with new meaning. Comedy uses this constantly, but so does drama.
* Bookends — beginning and ending mirror one another, except the character has changed.
* Plant and Payoff — quietly establish something, then cash it in later. Broader than Chekhov’s Gun.
* Deus ex machina — an outside force suddenly solves an otherwise unsolvable problem. Literally “god from the machine.”
* False Victory / False Defeat — it looks like they’ve won or lost, but the story has another layer.
* All Is Lost Moment — the point near the end where success appears impossible, usually immediately before the final transformation.
* Dark Night of the Soul — internal version of “all is lost”: identity/worldview collapses before reconstruction.
* Death and Rebirth — literal or symbolic death followed by emergence as a changed person.
* The Double / Doppelgänger — another character embodies what the protagonist could become, represses, fears, or lacks.
* Foil — a contrasting character makes another character’s qualities easier to see.
* Shadow — Jungian-style adversary representing denied or dangerous aspects of the hero.
* Trickster — disrupts rules, exposes assumptions, causes transformations through chaos. Loki, Bugs Bunny, many mythological figures.
* Everyman — deliberately ordinary character through whom the audience enters an extraordinary world.
* Unreliable Narrator — the person telling the story cannot be completely trusted.
* Frame Story — one story contains another story. Someone is telling us what happened.
* Story Within a Story — embedded narrative reflects, comments on, or alters the surrounding narrative.
* Prophecy — knowledge of a possible future shapes behavior, often helping cause the very outcome predicted.
* Self-Fulfilling Prophecy — believing something will happen changes behavior enough to make it happen.
* Pyrrhic Victory — you win, but the cost makes the victory almost indistinguishable from defeat.
* Faustian Bargain — gain something highly desirable by accepting a hidden or catastrophic price.
* Forbidden Knowledge — discovering the truth itself creates danger or transformation.
* Pandora’s Box — an irreversible action releases consequences that cannot simply be put back.
* Sword of Damocles — a looming threat remains suspended over otherwise successful characters.
* Achilles’ Heel — one specific vulnerability undermines otherwise exceptional strength.
* Trojan Horse — something enters disguised as benign and reveals its true function from inside.
* Cassandra — someone correctly sees disaster coming but cannot convince anyone.
* Sisyphus — endless work that resets or never reaches completion.
* Icarus — ambition/ascension exceeds limits and causes a fall.
* Orpheus — getting back what was lost, but failing because of one final act of doubt or disobedience.
* Prodigal Son — departure, degradation/discovery, return, reconciliation.
* Sacrificial Lamb — one suffers or dies so others can survive/change.
* Scapegoat — one person is loaded with blame belonging to the larger group/system.
* Beauty and the Beast — apparent monstrosity conceals value; relationship changes perception or reality.
* David and Goliath — weak-looking challenger defeats overwhelming incumbent through asymmetric advantage.
* Rags to Riches / Fall From Grace — large movement in social or moral state.
* Fish Out of Water — character enters a world whose rules they don’t understand, letting the audience learn alongside them.
* Odd Couple — incompatible personalities become effective precisely because of their differences.
* Enemy Mine — enemies must cooperate against a larger problem.
* Found Family — unrelated people become a functional family through shared experience.
* The Reveal — information causes previous events to be reinterpreted.
* Twist Ending — the final information changes the apparent meaning of the story.
* Rashomon Effect — multiple perspectives give incompatible accounts of the same event.
* Groundhog Day Loop — repetition allows learning through accumulated deltas while the environment resets.
* Ship of Theseus — gradual replacement raises the question of what preserves identity.
* Monkey’s Paw — a wish is technically granted but in a disastrous interpretation.
* Hobson’s Choice — an apparent choice where only one real option exists.
* Morton’s Fork — either choice leads to the same undesirable conclusion.
* Catch-22 — circular rules prevent escape because satisfying one requirement violates another.

And this is where they get particularly useful for Pantheon: these aren’t merely storytelling clichés. Many are compressed causal architectures.

Trojan Horse = transport through trusted interface.
Chekhov’s Gun = delayed affordance.
Groundhog Day = iterative learning with environmental reset.
MacGuffin = explicit objective generating implicit transformation.
Rashomon = multi-observer reconstruction under perspectival uncertainty.
Ship of Theseus = identity invariant under component replacement.
Cassandra = accurate signal + failed transmission channel.
Faustian bargain = local optimization concealing long-horizon cost.

Stories have been casually carrying around little algorithms for thousands of years.